Spring Boot + Karate API Testing — Steps Only
Step 1: Install Java and Maven
sudo apt update && sudo apt install openjdk-17-jdk maven -y

Step 2: Check Java
java -version

Step 3: Check Maven
mvn -version

Step 4: Create Spring Boot Project
Go to Spring Initializr and select:
- Project: Maven
- Language: Java
- Java: 17
- Dependency: Spring Web
- Dependency: Spring Boot Starter Test
Generate → Download ZIP → Extract → Open in VS Code.
Step 5: Create HelloController.java
Create:
src/main/java/com/example/demo/HelloController.java

Add:
package com.example.demo;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
public class HelloController {

    @GetMapping("/hello")
    public String hello() {
        return "Hello from Spring Boot!";
    }
}

Step 6: Run Spring Boot
mvn spring-boot:run

Step 7: Test REST API
Open a second terminal:
curl http://localhost:8080/hello

Step 8: Add Karate Dependency
Open pom.xml and add inside <dependencies>:
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.intuit.karate</groupId>
    <artifactId>karate-junit5</artifactId>
    <version>1.4.1</version>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>

Step 9: Create Test Folder
mkdir -p src/test/java/com/example/demo

Step 10: Create hello.feature
Create:
src/test/java/com/example/demo/hello.feature

Add:
Feature: Test Spring Boot API

Scenario: Test Hello API
    Given url 'http://localhost:8080/hello'
    When method GET
    Then status 200
    And match response == 'Hello from Spring Boot!'

Step 11: Create KarateTest.java
Create:
src/test/java/com/example/demo/KarateTest.java

Add:
package com.example.demo;

import com.intuit.karate.junit5.Karate;

class KarateTest {

    @Karate.Test
    Karate testHello() {
        return Karate.run("hello").relativeTo(getClass());
    }
}

Step 12: Run Automated Test
Keep Spring Boot running in Terminal 1.
In Terminal 2:
mvn test

Step 13: Verify
Check for:
BUILD SUCCESS
